  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn impressive late July rain storm continues across NY and New\nEngland. A closed/cutoff low continues to impact eastern NY and\nwestern New England with bands of stratiform rainfall with \nisolated embedded t-storms possible. Double-barrel low pressure\nsystem with one low over southern/eastern NY and a coastal low \nnear Long Island. Deep fetch of moisture continues from the \nAtlantic and Gulf with PWAT anomalies 2 to 3 STDEVs above normal\nin the 1.5-1.7\" range in the SPS RAP meso-analysis. Multiple \nembedded vortices are impacting the forecast area with one \nrecently moved through the western Mohawk Valley, and another \nmoving over the Capital Region with a third moving into NW CT \nand western MA. Periods of rain will continue with rainfall \nrates mainly 0.1-0.5\"/hr though closer to the heavier bands, \nsome 0.5-1.0\"/hr may briefly occur. Warm rainfall efficient\nprocesses continue in the deformation zone with high freezing\nlevels 12-13 kft AGL. Training rainfall echoes will also yield\nadditional flash flooding/flooding in spots. \n\nAn additional 0.5\"-2.0\" of rainfall is\npossible in the Flood Watch area...and some localized amounts to 3\"\nor so are possible along the east facing slopes of the eastern \nCatskills, southern Greens and Berkshires. Rainfall totals in \nthe Capital Region/Saratoga Region, upper Mid Hudson Valley, \nnorthern Taconics, Helderbergs, and the eastern Catskills have \nreceived 4-11\" of rainfall with 11.03\" of rainfall in the NYS \nmesonet at Schodack and 10.69\" at Kinderhook as of 2 pm. This \nrainfall has fallen in the last 24-30 hours. The Albany Airport \nhas a rainfall total of 5.95\" over the past 30 hours since 8 am \nyesterday until 2 pm, which is a very high amount for any \nrainfall event. The Flood Watch will remain up until 8 pm/00Z. \nWe want to keep it up for the afternoon/evening commute. Ponding\nof water on roadways, poor drainage flooding of low-lying and \nurban areas, and some road washouts may continue. Highs today \nwere mainly in the 60s, except for some lower to mid 70s over \nthe mid Hudson Valley and southern Taconics, where the rain let\nup with a few break in the clouds. \n\nSome drier air may feed into the storm system, as the showers\nwill lighten in intensity tonight and become more scattered \nacross portions of the region. Patchy fog may occur in some \nlocations. Lows will be mainly in the 50s to around 60F in a \nfew spots. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nSome minor to low end moderate river flooding continues to \noccur on the Esopus Creek at Mt Tremper/Cold Brook and Mt \nMarion. This is due to the rainfall of 4-9+\" and run-off in the\nbasins. Mt Tremper looks to have crested and these points could\nfall below flood stage by the late afternoon/early evening. \nSome additional minor/moderate flooding continues on the \nSchoharie Creek at Prattsville, Gilboa Bridge, and Breakabeen. \nWe are monitoring the potential for minor flooding at \nBurtonsville. We continue a Flood Warning for minor flooding at \nWilliamstown on the Hoosic River and potentially Eagle Bridge in\nRensselaer Co. Minor flooding of small streams and creeks \ncontinuing across portions of the Capital Region/Upper Mid \nHudson Valley/eastern Catskills/Helderbergs and the eastern \nMohawk Valley. The rivers/streams will continue to crest late \nthis afternoon through tonight. Any additional flooding on the \nmain stem rivers will depend where additional heavy rain occurs.\nWe continue to carefully monitor rivers and streams in western \nNew England where the heaviest rain may occur into tonight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nThe low pressure system and the mid and upper low slowly moves\neast of the region on Thu. Some scattered showers and isolated \nthunderstorms are possible with lighter rainfall amounts. Temps \nwill still run below normal with partly to mostly cloudy skies. \nHigh pressure begins to build in from the west/northwest from \nthe Ohio Valley and lower Great Lakes Region for Friday with \nmainly dry conditions aside from an isolated pop-up \nshower/thunderstorm over the western New England higher terrain\nand the eastern Adirondacks. Max temps will rise back into the \n70s to lower 80s with moderate humidity levels with dewpoints in\nthe 60s. The first day of August will feature typical \nsummertime warmth and humidity on Saturday with temps 80-85F in \nthe valleys...and 70s to around 80F over the hills and mtns with\ndry conditions. The drier weather should allow for streams, \ncreeks and rivers to recede into the weekend.",
